{"name":"Kumquat","hex":"#fb9942","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kumquat","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, orange citrus-like fruit which is native to Asia (Citrus japonica, syn. Fortunella japonica)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kumquat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kumquat","blocks":[{"text":"Kumquats, sometimes spelled cumquat, are a group of small, angiosperm, fruit-bearing trees in the family Rutaceae. The edible fruit closely resembles the orange in color, texture, and anatomy, but is much smaller, being approximately the size of a large olive. They are native to southern China but have been cultivated for centuries in Japan and Southeast Asia. The kumquat is a fairly cold-hardy citrus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Kumquat_from_Spain.jpg/330px-Kumquat_from_Spain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumquat","wikidata":"Q5122903"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Fort-hindsii.jpg/500px-Fort-hindsii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Fort-hindsii.jpg/1280px-Fort-hindsii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Fort-hindsii.jpg/500px-Fort-hindsii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFort-hindsii.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Starr-121108-0788-Fortunella_x_crassifolia-fruit-Pali_o_Waipio-Maui_%2825103025561%29.jpg/500px-Starr-121108-0788-Fortunella_x_crassifolia-fruit-Pali_o_Waipio-Maui_%2825103025561%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped